es","Economics","Energy Systems [Global Engagement]","English","Manufacturing Engineering","Mechanical Design","Mechanical Engineering","Thermal Engineering"],"cgpa":{"status":"unspecified"},"compensation":{"max_ctc_lpa":26.0},"offers":3,"test":null,"gd":"Yes","interviewRounds":null,"roleCategories":["Engineering"],"jobDescription":"Click here to view Job Description","shortlistingProcess":"","testAndInterview":"No written test for this profile. The interview process has 2 rounds, both offline with one panelist each - the first covers general mechanical fundamentals (fluids, strength of materials, kinematics of machines, etc.), and the second builds on the first panelist's feedback, often including simulation/FEA-based questions drawn from your resume. Interviewers are friendly and the atmosphere is comfortable. A GD round is also part of the process (see the GD column for details).","preparationMaterials":"Mechanical coursework notes and GATE material are the main preparation sources. For GD, browsing popular/abstract discussion topics and preparing a few points in advance helps. 204","suggestions":"Apply selectively to profiles where you have a genuine shortlisting chance rather than everything possible - spreading too thin adds unnecessary stress. Stay calm through the process, since multiple rounds and rejections can add pressure.","sourcePage":202},{"id":"slb-field-engineer-trainee-2026-27","company":"SLB","profile":"Field Engineer Trainee","location":"Mumbai/Barmer/Kakinada/Vadodara/Assam","year":"2026-27","eligibleAllBranches":true,"eligibleBranches":["Aerospace Engineering","Bio-Technology","Biological Engineering","Chemical Engineering","Civil Engineering","Computer Science and Engineering","Electrical Engineering","Engineering Design","IDDD - Advanced Materials and Nanotechnology","IDDD - Bio-Medical Engineering","IDDD - Complex Systems and Dynamics","IDDD - Computational Engineering","IDDD - Cyber Physical Systems","IDDD - Data Science","IDDD - Electric Vehicles","IDDD - Energy Systems","IDDD - Quantitative Finance","IDDD - Quantum Science & Technology","IDDD - Robotics","IDDD - Tech MBA (M.B.A.)","Mechanical Engineering","Metallurgical and Materials Engineering","Naval Architecture and Ocean Engineering","Physics (M.S.)"],"cgpa":{"status":"unspecified"},"compensation":{"max_ctc_lpa":34.0},"offers":1,"test":null,"gd":"Yes","interviewRounds":null,"roleCategories":["Engineering"],"jobDescription":"Click here to view Job Description","shortlistingProcess":"","testAndInterview":"A GD round comes first - around 100 students are shortlisted based on resumes, 12 per GD group, with fairly random/abstract topics (e.g., '1%'). Think on your feet and try to steer the discussion, approaching the topic from multiple angles. This is followed by 1 offline interview round with 2 panelists, mostly resume-based with a few basic HR questions - course projects aren't covered in much depth. Positions of Responsibility (PoRs) are useful for answering HR-style questions.","preparationMaterials":"No specific external resources are needed beyond a well-prepared resume; using an AI 208 tool (e.g., a deep-research query) to get a sense of generic GD topics asked at IITs can help.","suggestions":"Stay confident and maintain a composed, continuous tone through the interview. Interviewers may try to flip your positive points (like CGPA) into a negative - don't fall for it, stick to your stance. Keep a good smile throughout.","sourcePage":206},{"id":"speakx-ai-associate-product-manager-2026-27","company":"SpeakX.ai","profile":"Associate Product Manager","location":"Gurgaon, Haryana","year":"2026-27","eligibleAllBranches":false,"eligibleBranches":["Aerospace Engineering","Chemical Engineering","Civil Engineering","Computer Science and Engg.","Computer Science and Engineering","Electrical Engineering","Engineering Design","IDDD - Complex Systems and Dynamics","IDDD - Computational Engineering","IDDD - Data Science","IDDD - Quantitative Finance","IDDD - Tech MBA (M.B.A.)","Mechanical Engineering"],"cgpa":{"status":"unspecified"},"compensation":{"max_ctc_lpa":5.0},"offers":1,"test":null,"gd":"No GD.","interviewRounds":null,"roleCategories":["Product"],"jobDescription":"Click here to view Job Description","shortlistingProcess":"","testAndInterview":"An aptitude test (1.5 hours, easy-to-moderate, +4/-1 marking, with a large number of questions - aim to attempt as many as possible) is followed by 3 interview rounds, each taken by a different interviewer, of moderate difficulty. One round is a case-study round (moderate difficulty, standard case-interview format - break the problem into parts; there's no single correct answer, and interviewers are mainly assessing structured thinking and creativity). Interviewers are generally friendly. There is no GD round for this profile.","preparationMaterials":"For the aptitude test: practice online aptitude/logical-reasoning questions, including past CAT papers (actual difficulty tends to be milder). For the case-study round: the IITM Casebook and Aaditya Agarwal's case-study prep videos on YouTube.","suggestions":"Prepare well and stay confident - being calm and thinking rationally under pressure is often the deciding factor between landing the offer or not.","sourcePage":209},{"id":"squarepoint-desk-quant-analyst-2026-27","company":"Squarepoint","profile":"Desk Quant Analyst","location":"Bengaluru","year":"2026-27","eligibleAllBranches":true,"eligibleBranches":[],"cgpa":{"status":"known","min":7.5},"compensation":{"max_ctc_lpa":66.13882},"offers":1,"test":null,"gd":"Yes","interviewRounds":null,"roleCategories":["ML / AI"],"jobDescription":"Click here to view Job Description","shortlistingProcess":"","testAndInterview":"Online Assessment (HackerRank): one easy-medium and one hard DSA/CP-style question. The interview process then has 3 rounds - Round 1: 3 DSA questions 213 (easy/medium LeetCode-level) after a brief introduction, taken by a relatively junior interviewer. Round 2: a deep dive into your quant internship, basics (and slightly beyond) of Linear Regression, 2 medium DSA/LeetCode questions, and 2 easy-medium probability questions (Heard-on-the-Street / '50 Problems' style), taken by a more experienced interviewer. Round 3: mostly an HR-style round with a senior Quant Trader, covering your quant internship and general HR questions (why Squarepoint, etc.).","preparationMaterials":"DSA: Striver's A2Z and SDE sheets, plus some questions from the CP-31 sheet (TLE Eliminators). Probability: PuzzledQuant, '50 Problems in Probability', Brain Stellar, and Heard on the Street.","suggestions":"Put real effort into your resume. Some shortlists can be somewhat random, so don't be discouraged if you don't get many - that part is outside your control, but test performance is, so give it your all there.","sourcePage":212},{"id":"stripe-software-engineering-new-grad-2026-27","company":"Stripe","profile":"Software Engineering, New Grad","location":"Bangalore","year":"2026-27","eligibleAllBranches":true,"eligibleBranches":[],"cgpa":{"status":"unspecified"},"compensation":{"max_ctc_lpa":30.866},"offers":4,"test":null,"gd":"No GD","interviewRounds":null,"roleCategories":["Software"],"jobDescription":"Click here to view Job Description","shortlistingProcess":"","testAndInterview":"217 2 screening stages before interviews: an online HackerRank assessment (~1 hour, one multi-part question building progressively - closer to real work problems than typical LeetCode/Codeforces style), followed by a face-to-face online round in the same format (parts revealed progressively; you can look up resources online but not use LLMs/AI agents). The interview process then has 3 rounds: Round 1 mirrors the second screening round but in person; Round 2 is a 'bug squash' round - you're given a repo with failing test cases and need to debug it (the focus is on your debugging approach, not just whether you fix it); Round 3 is a non-technical manager chat about your work